{"name":"Martian Moon","hex":"#c3e9d3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"martian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Martian (“of or pertaining to the planet Mars”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Martian packet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/martian"},{"term":"moon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Moon (“the Earth's only permanent natural satellite”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any natural satellite of a planet.","The light received from the Moon; moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moons of Mars","blocks":[{"text":"The two moons of Mars are Phobos and Deimos. They are irregular in shape. Both were discovered by American astronomer Asaph Hall in August 1877 and are named after the Greek mythological twin characters Phobos and Deimos who accompanied their father Ares into battle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Phobos_colour_2008.jpg/330px-Phobos_colour_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moons_of_Mars","wikidata":"Q177268"},{"title":"Mars in fiction","blocks":[{"text":"Mars, the fourth planet from the Sun, has appeared as a setting in works of fiction since at least the mid-1600s. Trends in the planet's portrayal have largely been influenced by advances in planetary science. It became the most popular celestial object in fiction in the late 1800s, when it became clear that there was no life on the Moon. The predominant genre depicting Mars at the time was utopian fiction. Around the same time, the mistaken belief that there are canals on Mars emerged and made its way into fiction, popularized by Percival Lowell's speculations of an ancient civilization having constructed them. The War of the Worlds, H. G. Wells's novel about an alien invasion of Earth by sinister Martians, was published in 1897 and went on to have a major influence on the science fiction genre."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/The_War_of_the_Worlds_by_Henrique_Alvim_Corr%C3%AAa%2C_original_graphic_15.jpg/330px-The_War_of_the_Worlds_by_Henrique_Alvim_Corr%C3%AAa%2C_original_graphic_15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_in_fiction","wikidata":"Q2661218"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Deimos-MRO.jpg/500px-Deimos-MRO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Deimos-MRO.jpg/500px-Deimos-MRO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeimos-MRO.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/PIA17352-MarsMoons-PhobosPassesDeimos-RealTime.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/PIA17352-MarsMoons-PhobosPassesDeimos-RealTime.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Phobos passing in front of Deimos, as viewed in real time by the Curiosity rover on the surface of Mars.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APIA17352-MarsMoons-PhobosPassesDeimos-RealTime.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/HallPortrait.jpg/500px-HallPortrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/HallPortrait.jpg/1280px-HallPortrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/HallPortrait.jpg/500px-HallPortrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Asaph Hall III, discoverer of Phobos and Deimos","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHallPortrait.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Usno-telescope-equalized-1.png/500px-Usno-telescope-equalized-1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Usno-telescope-equalized-1.png/1280px-Usno-telescope-equalized-1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Usno-telescope-equalized-1.png/500px-Usno-telescope-equalized-1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The telescope used by Asaph Hall in the discovery of the Martian moons","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUsno-telescope-equalized-1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/PIA17351-ApparentSizes-MarsDeimosPhobos-EarthMoon.jpg/250px-PIA17351-ApparentSizes-MarsD